### Discussed in https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind/discussions/6169

<div type='discussions-op-text'>

<sup>Originally posted by **margarita-sk** August 20, 2026</sup>
When configuring JsonMapper with null handling:

```
        .changeDefaultNullHandling(v -> v.withValueNulls(Nulls.SKIP))
        .changeDefaultNullHandling(v -> v.withContentNulls(Nulls.SKIP))
```
the configuration appears to have no effect on properties handled by @JsonAnySetter.

### Reproduction
```
class Jackson3Test {

    @Test
    void testSkipNullValuesInJsonAnySetter() {
        JsonMapper jsonMapper = JsonMapper.builder()
                .changeDefaultNullHandling(v -> v.withValueNulls(Nulls.SKIP))
                .changeDefaultNullHandling(v -> v.withContentNulls(Nulls.SKIP))
                .build();

        String json = """
                {
                "non_null_property": "test",
                "null_property": null
                }
                """;
        TestPojo input = jsonMapper.readValue(json, TestPojo.class);

        Assertions.assertEquals(1, input.getDynamicProperties()
                .size()); // is failed, but expected to pass because of the default null handling configuration
    }
    
    static class TestPojo {

        private final Map<String, JsonNode> dynamicProperties = new HashMap<>();

        @JsonAnySetter
        public void setDynamicProperties(String key, JsonNode value) {
            this.dynamicProperties.put(key, value);
        }

        public Map<String, JsonNode> getDynamicProperties() {
            return this.dynamicProperties;
        }

    }

}
```

### Actual behavior
The @JsonAnySetter method is invoked for both properties, including the property whose value is null.

### Expected behavior
With the mapper configured with:
```
        .changeDefaultNullHandling(v -> v.withValueNulls(Nulls.SKIP))
        .changeDefaultNullHandling(v -> v.withContentNulls(Nulls.SKIP))
```
I would expect the null_property value to be skipped and the @JsonAnySetter method not to be invoked for it.

### Question
Is the current behavior intentional for @JsonAnySetter?
If this is intentional, is there currently a recommended way to configure @JsonAnySetter to skip null values?
If it is not intentional, is support for applying Nulls.SKIP to @JsonAnySetter planned for Jackson 3?</div>

With a `@JsonFormat(shape = ARRAY)` builder that has a `@JsonCreator`, a non-creator property that comes before the last creator property is silently lost if the builder is immutable. There is no error: the property keeps its default value.

This is the property-based counterpart of #6363.

## Reproduction

```java
@JsonDeserialize(builder = Value.Builder.class)
@JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.ARRAY)
static class Value {
    final int a, c;

    Value(int a, int c) {
        this.a = a;
        this.c = c;
    }

    @JsonPropertyOrder({ "a", "c" })
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.ARRAY)
    static class Builder {
        final int a, c;

        @JsonCreator
        Builder(@JsonProperty("c") int c) {
            this(0, c);
        }

        private Builder(int a, int c) {
            this.a = a;
            this.c = c;
        }

        public Builder withA(int v) { return new Builder(v, c); }
        public Value build() { return new Value(a, c); }
    }
}

Value v = JsonMapper.builder().build().readValue("[5,6]", Value.class);
// expected: a=5, c=6
// actual:   a=0, c=6
```

The same input gives `a=5, c=6` if `withA()` mutates the builder and returns `this`. Both results are from `3.x` plus #6391.

## Likely cause

`BeanAsArrayBuilderDeserializer._deserializeUsingPropertyBased()` buffers `a` because the builder doesn't exist yet. `PropertyBasedCreator.build()` then applies buffered values with `pv.assign(ctxt, bean)` and always returns the original instance, so the new builder returned by `withA()` appears to be dropped. I confirmed the loss but did not trace each step in a debugger.

`PropertyBasedCreator.build()` is shared code, so other property-based builder paths may need checking too. Only the array-shaped path was tested.

If a `DeserializationProblemHandler` returns `null` from `handleUnexpectedToken()` for non-array input to a `@JsonFormat(shape = ARRAY)` builder type, deserialization fails with a `ValueInstantiationException` wrapping a `NullPointerException`. A handler returning `null` should produce a `null` result, which is what the equivalent POJO type (without a builder) does.

## Reproduction

```java
@JsonDeserialize(builder = Value.Builder.class)
@JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.ARRAY)
static class Value {
    int x;

    @JsonPropertyOrder({ "x" })
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.ARRAY)
    static class Builder {
        int x;

        public Builder withX(int v) { x = v; return this; }
        public Value build() {
            Value r = new Value();
            r.x = x;
            return r;
        }
    }
}

ObjectMapper mapper = JsonMapper.builder()
        .addHandler(new DeserializationProblemHandler() {
            @Override
            public Object handleUnexpectedToken(DeserializationContext ctxt, JavaType targetType,
                    JsonToken t, JsonParser p, String failureMsg) {
                p.skipChildren();
                return null;
            }
        })
        .build();

mapper.readValue("""
        {"x":1}""", Value.class);
```

Actual result, on `3.x` plus #6391:

```
ValueInstantiationException: Cannot construct instance of `Value$Builder`, problem: `java.lang.NullPointerException`
```

Expected result: `null`.

## Cause

`BeanAsArrayBuilderDeserializer.deserialize()` passes the handler's result straight to `finishBuild()`, which invokes the build method on it:

```java
if (!p.isExpectedStartArrayToken()) {
    return finishBuild(ctxt, _deserializeFromNonArray(p, ctxt));
}
```

A null check is needed before calling `finishBuild()`.

