### Describe the bug

In 3.x, `JsonValueSerializer` does not check its `PropertySerializerMap` before resolving a serializer for a dynamically typed `@JsonValue` result (for example `@JsonValue Object`). It calls `_findAndAddDynamic()` directly, in both `_findSerializer()` (used by `serialize()`) and `serializeWithType()`.

`PropertySerializerMap.findAndAdd*()` and `newWith()` do not look for an existing entry, which has two effects:

1. Every value goes through serializer lookup and `createContextual()` again, even if its type has been seen before.
2. Every call adds another entry for the same type. Once the map reaches its limit of 8 entries (`emptyForProperties()` does not reset when full), it stops accepting new ones, so any other type seen later is never cached.

2.x is not affected: `_findDynamicSerializer()` there calls `serializerFor()` first.

### Version information

Observed on `3.1` (3.1.9-SNAPSHOT). From reading the code, the same calls without a lookup are on `3.0`, `3.2` and `3.x`.

### To Reproduce

```java
static class V {
    final Object o;
    V(Object o) { this.o = o; }
    @JsonValue public Object val() { return o; }
}
static class A { }
static class B { }

static class Counting<T> extends StdSerializer<T> {
    final Map<String, Integer> counts;
    final Class<T> type;
    Counting(Class<T> type, Map<String, Integer> counts) {
        super(type);
        this.type = type;
        this.counts = counts;
    }
    @Override
    public ValueSerializer<?> createContextual(SerializationContext ctxt, BeanProperty p) {
        counts.merge(type.getSimpleName(), 1, Integer::sum);
        return this;
    }
    @Override
    public void serialize(T v, JsonGenerator g, SerializationContext ctxt) {
        g.writeString("x");
    }
}

Map<String, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>();
SimpleModule mod = new SimpleModule();
mod.addSerializer(A.class, new Counting<>(A.class, counts));
mod.addSerializer(B.class, new Counting<>(B.class, counts));
ObjectMapper mapper = JsonMapper.builder().addModule(mod).build();

List<V> as = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < 20; ++i) as.add(new V(new A()));
mapper.writeValueAsString(as);
// counts: {A=20}

List<V> bs = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) bs.add(new V(new B()));
mapper.writeValueAsString(bs);
// counts: {A=20, B=5}
```

### Expected behavior

Each type is contextualized once per `JsonValueSerializer` instance: `{A=1, B=1}`. Output is the same either way; the difference is redundant work and lost caching.

### Additional context

PR #6380 fixes the same problem in `StdConvertingSerializer`, by adding the `serializerFor()` lookup.

Things to consider for a fix:

- **Where to fix:** `JsonValueSerializer` could look up the map first, as `MapSerializer` and `CollectionSerializer` do, or the check could go into `StdDynamicSerializer._findAndAddDynamic()`. Note that `_findSerializer()` also passes a transformer that applies `@JsonIgnoreProperties`.
- **Behavior change:** contextualizing on every call currently means the `ObjectWriter` settings in effect are picked up each time. With caching, the first writer's settings stay in use, for example the time zone and locale captured for a `@JsonFormat(pattern = ...)` date. Properties without a converter already behave this way (a `Date`, `Object` or `List<Object>` property keeps the first writer's time zone), and #6380 does the same for converters. This may matter when choosing which branch to fix on.
- **Possibly related:** `EnumSetSerializer` also calls `_findAndAddDynamic()` without a lookup, once per `serialize()` call when `_elementSerializer` is `null`. This comes from reading the code only; I have not reproduced it.

