A property-level `@JsonIgnoreProperties` (or `@JsonIncludeProperties`) on a `Map`-valued `@JsonAnyGetter` filters the emitted entries, but the same annotation on an `ObjectNode`/`JsonNode`-valued any-getter (added in [databind#3604]) is silently ignored, so keys the annotation exists to drop are written to the output.

The Map case works because its `MapSerializer` reads the property-level ignoral/inclusion in `createContextual()` and skips matching keys. A node-valued any-getter has no `MapSerializer`, and `AnyGetterWriter` writes every `ObjectNode` entry without consulting those rules.

### Version

3.3.0-SNAPSHOT (also 3.1 / 3.2)

### Reproduction

```java
static class NodeBean {
    public int a = 1;

    @JsonIgnoreProperties("secret")
    @JsonAnyGetter
    public ObjectNode extra;
}

ObjectMapper mapper = JsonMapper.builder().build();
NodeBean bean = new NodeBean();
bean.extra = mapper.createObjectNode();
bean.extra.put("keep", 1);
bean.extra.put("secret", 2);

// expected: {"a":1,"keep":1}
// actual:   {"a":1,"keep":1,"secret":2}
System.out.println(mapper.writeValueAsString(bean));
```

The equivalent `Map<String,Object>` any-getter with the same annotation correctly emits `{"a":1,"keep":1}`. `@JsonIncludeProperties` shows the same divergence.

### Describe the bug

When a `Converter` declares `Object` as its output type (`StdConverter<Input, Object>`, for example), `StdConvertingSerializer` can only find the delegate serializer at runtime, from the class of the converted value.

It does this for every value: `_findSerializer()` looks up the serializer and runs `createContextual()` on it each time, even if the same class was just handled by the same serializer instance. With `@JsonInclude(NON_EMPTY)` this happens twice for a single value, once for `isEmpty()` and once for `serialize()`.

The class has a `PropertySerializerMap` (`_dynamicValueSerializers`) that appears intended to avoid this, but `_findSerializer()` never reads from it. It only calls `findAndAddSecondarySerializer()`, which does not check for an existing entry, so each call also adds another entry for the same class until the map reaches its limit of 8.

Output is correct. The problem is repeated work on every value, plus a map that is written to but never used. I have not measured how much this costs in practice; it will depend on what the delegate serializer's `createContextual()` does.

### Version information

Reproduced on `3.x` at 71c318818 (3.3.0-SNAPSHOT). The same `_findSerializer()` code is on `3.1`.

### To Reproduce

```java
@JsonSerialize(converter = ToObject.class)
static class Input {
    final Object value;
    Input(Object value) { this.value = value; }
}

static class ToObject extends StdConverter<Input, Object> {
    @Override public Object convert(Input in) { return in.value; }
}

static class Payload { }

static class Wrapper {
    @JsonInclude(JsonInclude.Include.NON_EMPTY)
    public Input value = new Input(new Payload());
}

static class CountingSerializer extends StdSerializer<Payload> {
    final Map<String, Integer> calls;
    CountingSerializer(Map<String, Integer> calls) {
        super(Payload.class);
        this.calls = calls;
    }
    @Override
    public ValueSerializer<?> createContextual(SerializationContext ctxt, BeanProperty prop) {
        calls.merge("createContextual", 1, Integer::sum);
        return this;
    }
    @Override
    public void serialize(Payload v, JsonGenerator g, SerializationContext ctxt) {
        g.writeString("payload");
    }
}

Map<String, Integer> calls = new TreeMap<>();
ObjectMapper mapper = JsonMapper.builder()
        .addModule(new SimpleModule().addSerializer(Payload.class, new CountingSerializer(calls)))
        .build();

mapper.writeValueAsString(List.of(
        new Input(new Payload()), new Input(new Payload()), new Input(new Payload())));
// ["payload","payload","payload"]
// calls: {createContextual=3}

calls.clear();
mapper.writeValueAsString(new Wrapper());
// {"value":"payload"}
// calls: {createContextual=2}
```

### Expected behavior

Repeated values of the same class should not need the delegate serializer to be looked up and contextualized each time, as far as that can be done without changing output (see below).

### Additional context

Contextualizing on every call also has a visible effect that output currently depends on: settings of the current `ObjectWriter` are picked up each time. For a property with `@JsonFormat(pattern = "HH:mm")` and a `Date` to `Object` converter, writing `new Date(0L)` with one mapper gives:

```
writer.with(UTC)         "when":"00:00"
writer.with(Asia/Tokyo)  "when":"09:00"
```

Properties without a converter behave differently: a `Date`, `Object` or `List<Object>` property with the same annotation keeps the first writer's time zone, giving `00:00` for both.

A single `StdConvertingSerializer` instance can also be shared between mappers, if it is created directly and registered through a module. Each mapper must keep using its own serializers.

PR #6380 first raised this problem. #6392 describes the same missing lookup in `JsonValueSerializer`.

