### Describe the bug

When a token that is neither `PROPERTY_NAME` nor `END_OBJECT` appears where a property name is expected inside an Object, two `BeanDeserializer` loops stop reading without reporting it. They return the value and leave the parser in the middle of the Object:

1. **Property-based creator loop** (records and `@JsonCreator` classes): `_deserializeUsingPropertyBased` loops `for (; t == JsonToken.PROPERTY_NAME; t = p.nextToken())`. Any other token ends the loop, and the bean is built as if `END_OBJECT` had been seen. The other property-based loops (unwrapped, external type id) have the same shape.
2. **Vanilla loop after an unknown property** (`MapperFeature.DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION` enabled, no unknown-property failure): `_vanillaDeserializeWithUnknown` has `if (ix != PropertyNameMatcher.MATCH_UNKNOWN_NAME) { return bean; }`.

All other loops report such a token through `_handleUnexpectedWithin()`. That includes `_vanillaDeserialize` before any unknown property, `deserializeFromObject`, the view loop and the builder loops.

JSON input cannot produce this token sequence, but a non-JSON parser or a `TokenBuffer` can. At the root, `FAIL_ON_TRAILING_TOKENS` (enabled by default in 3.x) catches the leftovers, but with a misleading "Trailing token" message. When the value is nested, or with `FAIL_ON_TRAILING_TOKENS` disabled, the enclosing deserializer reads the leftover tokens as its own, and data is lost silently.

### Version Information

3.3.0-SNAPSHOT (`3.x` as of 2026-10-30). The same loops exist in 2.x.

### Reproduction

```java
public record Rec(String s, int i) { }
public static class Pojo { public String s; public int i; }
public static class Wrapper { public Rec rec; public String after; }

// {"s":"a", ["x":1,] 3, "i":5}: a bare number where a property name belongs
static void inner(TokenBuffer tb, boolean unknown) {
    tb.writeStartObject();
    tb.writeName("s"); tb.writeString("a");
    if (unknown) { tb.writeName("x"); tb.writeNumber(1); }
    tb.writeNumber(3);
    tb.writeName("i"); tb.writeNumber(5);
    tb.writeEndObject();
}

ObjectMapper m = JsonMapper.builder()
        .disable(DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES,
                DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_TRAILING_TOKENS)
        .build();

// Record, nested: {"rec":{"s":"a",3,"i":5},"after":"z"}
TokenBuffer tb = TokenBuffer.forGeneration();
tb.writeStartObject();
tb.writeName("rec"); inner(tb, false);
tb.writeName("after"); tb.writeString("z");
tb.writeEndObject();
try (JsonParser p = tb.asParser(ObjectReadContext.empty())) {
    Wrapper w = m.readerFor(Wrapper.class).readValue(p);
    // w.rec == Rec[s=a, i=0], w.after == null
}

// POJO with DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION, after an unknown property
ObjectMapper vanilla = m.rebuild().enable(MapperFeature.DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION).build();
tb = TokenBuffer.forGeneration();
inner(tb, true);
try (JsonParser p = tb.asParser(ObjectReadContext.empty())) {
    Pojo pojo = vanilla.readerFor(Pojo.class).readValue(p);
    // pojo.s == "a", pojo.i == 0: no failure
}
```

Results on 3.x:

| Case | Result |
|---|---|
| record, root | `Rec[s=a, i=0]` |
| record, nested in `Wrapper` | `Wrapper[rec=Rec[s=a, i=0], after=null]`: `"i":5` is consumed by `Wrapper` as an unknown property, and the inner `END_OBJECT` closes `Wrapper` |
| POJO, default config | `MismatchedInputException: Cannot deserialize value of type Pojo from Integer value` (expected) |
| POJO, `DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION`, no unknown property | same `MismatchedInputException` (expected) |
| POJO, `DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION`, after unknown property | `Pojo[s=a, i=0]` |

With `FAIL_ON_TRAILING_TOKENS` left enabled, the silent cases fail at the root with "Trailing token (`JsonToken.PROPERTY_NAME`) found after value", which points away from the actual problem.

### Expected behavior

The same as the other loops: report the token through `_handleUnexpectedWithin()` (`DeserializationContext.handleUnexpectedToken`), so that a `DeserializationProblemHandler` can still handle it and the parser is never left in the middle of an Object.

### Additional context

Found while making jackson-module-blackbird's generated readers behave exactly like stock; Blackbird currently copies this behavior on purpose and would follow a fix here.

#6174 made `ThrowableDeserializer` honor `@JsonView`, and exempted the standard `Throwable` properties from that filtering via `_isStandardThrowableProperty()`. The exemption is necessary, but it keys on the property's *identity* alone, so it cannot tell "this property has no `@JsonView` and was defaulted out of every view" from "the user explicitly annotated this property" — and silently overrides the latter.

### Why the exemption exists

`Throwable`'s own properties carry no `@JsonView`. In `BeanDeserializerFactory.addBeanProps()`:

```java
Class<?>[] views = propDef.findViews();
if (views == null) {
    views = beanDesc.findDefaultViews();
}
prop.setViews(views);
```

and `BasicBeanDescription.findDefaultViews()` forces `NO_VIEWS` (an empty array) when `MapperFeature.DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION` is disabled — which is the default. `ViewMatcher.construct()` maps an empty array to `ViewMatcher.EMPTY`, whose `isVisibleForView()` always returns `false`. So without the exemption, `cause` and `stackTrace` would be excluded from *every* active view, and `@JsonView` would effectively break `Throwable` deserialization.

### The problem

Because the exemption is unconditional, an explicit `@JsonView` on one of those properties has no effect:

```java
static class Public {}
static class Internal {}

static class Ex extends RuntimeException {
    public Ex() { super(); }

    @Override @JsonView(Internal.class)
    public void setStackTrace(StackTraceElement[] st) { super.setStackTrace(st); }

    @Override @JsonView(Internal.class)
    public StackTraceElement[] getStackTrace() { return super.getStackTrace(); }
}

Ex ex = MAPPER.readerWithView(Public.class).forType(Ex.class)
    .readValue("{\"stackTrace\":[{\"className\":\"some.Class\",\"methodName\":\"m\",\"fileName\":\"C.java\",\"lineNumber\":42}]}");

ex.getStackTrace().length;  // 1 -- set from input despite the Internal-only view
```

Expected: the property is not populated under the `Public` view, since it was explicitly restricted to `Internal`.

This is the same class of leak #6174 set out to fix, just via a different route, and it is what a user gets when they deliberately try to keep a stack trace out of a public view.

### Suggested direction

The distinction is available where views are assigned, not where they are read: at that point `propDef.findViews() == null` says the property was defaulted, rather than annotated. Options include not forcing `NO_VIEWS` onto the standard `Throwable` properties in the first place, or recording that the views were defaulted so the read-time exemption can apply only to those. Either keeps #6174's behavior for un-annotated properties while letting an explicit annotation win.

### Notes

- Pre-existing: same result before and after #6174 — that change gave the leak a specific mechanism but did not introduce it.
- Unaffected by #6189 (which fixes how the names are *matched*, not the identity-vs-annotation question).
- Narrow: it requires deliberately overriding a `Throwable` accessor just to annotate it. Filing mostly so the limitation is recorded rather than rediscovered.

`BeanDeserializer` wraps non-Jackson exceptions from most property handling in a `DatabindException`, with the property as its path, unless `DeserializationFeature.WRAP_EXCEPTIONS` is disabled. Exceptions from handling of unknown properties are the exception to this: one thrown by a `DeserializationProblemHandler` escapes as it is, with no information on the property or bean.

## Reproduction

```java
DeserializationProblemHandler handler = new DeserializationProblemHandler() {
    @Override
    public boolean handleUnknownProperty(DeserializationContext ctxt, JsonParser p,
            ValueDeserializer<?> deser, Object beanOrClass, String propName) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("handler failure");
    }
};
ObjectMapper mapper = JsonMapper.builder().addHandler(handler).build();
mapper.readValue("{\"zzz\":1}", Pojo.class); // throws IllegalStateException
```

Results on `3.x` at `08233463c`:

| Failure | Input | Thrown |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Problem handler, POJO, default settings | `{"zzz":1}` | raw `IllegalStateException` |
| Problem handler, POJO, `DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION` enabled | `{"zzz":1}` | raw `IllegalStateException` |
| Problem handler, record | `{"a":1,"zzz":1}` | raw `IllegalStateException` |
| Any-setter | `{"zzz":1}` | `DatabindException`, path `AnyBean["zzz"]` |
| Setter | `{"a":1}` | `DatabindException`, path `BadSetter["a"]` |

## Proposed change

Wrap these exceptions in `BeanDeserializerBase.handleUnknownProperty()`, using `wrapAndThrow()`:

```java
try {
    super.handleUnknownProperty(p, ctxt, beanOrClass, propName);
} catch (JacksonException e) { // has its path already
    throw e;
} catch (Exception e) {
    throw wrapAndThrow(e, (beanOrClass == null) ? handledType() : beanOrClass, propName, ctxt);
}
```

`JacksonException`s need to pass through as they are, since `wrapAndThrow()` adds a path segment and the path would become `["zzz","zzz"]`. With `WRAP_EXCEPTIONS` disabled, `wrapAndThrow()` rethrows the original exception, so that setting keeps today's behaviour.

This changes behaviour for code that catches a specific exception type thrown from its problem handler, so it may need to wait for a minor version.

