Explicit `@JsonFormat(shape = NUMBER_INT)` / `NUMBER_FLOAT` (or config override) is not honored consistently for nanosecond handling of `java.time` values.

### Serialization

`DurationSerializer`, `LocalTimeSerializer`, `LocalDateTimeSerializer` and `OffsetTimeSerializer` drop the `shape` argument in their `withFormat()` override, so `useNanoseconds()` (which maps `NUMBER_INT` -> no nanos, `NUMBER_FLOAT` -> nanos) never sees it. `InstantSerializer` (and `OffsetDateTime`/`ZonedDateTime` serializers) do retain the shape. With default `JsonMapper`:

```java
static class IntBean {
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.NUMBER_INT)
    public Duration d = Duration.ofSeconds(3, 500_000_000);
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.NUMBER_INT)
    public Instant i = Instant.ofEpochSecond(3, 500_000_000);
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.NUMBER_INT)
    public LocalTime t = LocalTime.of(1, 2, 3, 500_000_000);
}
```

produces

```json
{"d":3.500000000,"i":3500,"t":[1,2,3,500000000]}
```

`Instant` honors `NUMBER_INT` and writes millis; `Duration` and `LocalTime` still write nanoseconds.

### Deserialization

No deserializer considers shape: only `DateTimeFeature.READ_DATE_TIMESTAMPS_AS_NANOSECONDS` (or per-property `JsonFormat.Feature`). As a result `Instant` with `shape = NUMBER_INT` does not round-trip under default settings: `3500` (millis) is read back as `1970-01-01T00:58:20Z` (seconds).

Fixing only the serializer side would break round-trip for the other types as well (e.g. `Duration` written as `3500`, read back as `PT58M20S`), so both sides need to change together.

### Suggested fix

- Serializers: pass `shape` through in `withFormat()`
- Deserializers: use explicit numeric shape to determine whether timestamps are read as nanoseconds

with the same precedence in both directions: explicit `JsonFormat.Feature` > shape > global `DateTimeFeature`.

Since this changes output (and input handling) for explicitly annotated values, it should target 3.3.0.

