// fails: "Object Id conflict: Id [ObjectId: key=7, ...] already bound to an Object"
mapper.readValue("""
    {"foo":{"id":1,"name":"a"},
     "plain":[{"id":7,"name":"b"},{"id":7,"name":"c"}]}
    """, Holder4.class);

// also fails afterwards, even though `Foo4` has no Object Id annotations:
mapper.readValue("""
    [{"id":7,"name":"b"},{"id":7,"name":"c"}]
    """, Foo4[].class);
```

Order does not matter: reading `Foo4[]` first (to construct the plain deserializer), then `Holder4`, then `Foo4[]` again also fails on the last call.

### Expected behavior

Elements of `plain` (and `Foo4` values elsewhere) are deserialized without Object Id handling; only `Holder4.foo` uses it.

### Proposed fix

In the in-place branch of `withProperty()`, copy `_propsInOrder`, replace the entry in the copy and return a new `BeanPropertyMap` (with same `_aliasDefs`).

### Description

Module auto-discovery (`MapperBuilder.findAndAddModules()` / `findModules()`) is a plain `ServiceLoader<JacksonModule>` scan: every module listed in `META-INF/services/tools.jackson.databind.JacksonModule` (or declared via `provides` in `module-info.java`) gets registered, unconditionally.

A module has no way to take part in that decision. The only way to opt out is to not advertise itself via SPI at all, which is all-or-nothing and fixed at build time. There is no way for a module to, for example:

* decline auto-registration when some optional runtime dependency it needs is not available,
* decline auto-registration based on configuration (system property etc.), or
* be advertised via SPI but require explicit registration (for example because it needs configuration that the no-arg constructor cannot provide sensibly).

### Describe the solution you'd like

Add a new method to `JacksonModule`:

```java
/**
 * Method called to determine whether this module can be registered
 * automatically, when found via module auto-discovery
 * ({@link MapperBuilder#findModules()}, {@link MapperBuilder#findAndAddModules()}).
 * If {@code false} is returned, module is skipped by auto-discovery; it can
 * still be registered explicitly with {@link MapperBuilder#addModule}.
 *<p>
 * Default implementation returns {@code true}.
 *
 * @since 3.4
 */
public boolean canAutoRegister() {
    return true;
}
```

and have `MapperBuilder.findModules(ClassLoader)` (and so `findAndAddModules()`) skip modules for which it returns `false`.

Explicit registration (`addModule()` / `addModules()`) would ignore this method: it only affects auto-discovery. Module dependencies (`getDependencies()`) of explicitly or automatically registered modules would also be unaffected.

Default of `true` keeps existing behavior for all current modules.

### Usage example

```java
public class MyModule extends SimpleModule {
    @Override
    public boolean canAutoRegister() {
        // only auto-register if optional dependency is present
        try {
            Class.forName("com.example.OptionalLib", false, getClass().getClassLoader());
            return true;
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

### Additional context

Naming: `canAutoRegister()` rather than `willAutoRegister()`, since the module does not register itself; it only indicates whether it is eligible for automatic registration, and the caller (auto-discovery) decides.

An alternative would be a filtering overload like `MapperBuilder.findAndAddModules(Predicate<JacksonModule>)`, which lets the caller (not the module) decide; the two are complementary.

