### Describe the bug

Serializers for `EnumSet` values (and for the enums inside them) are looked up and contextualized again for every value, instead of once. Output is correct. The cost is redundant serializer lookups and contextualization, plus dynamic serializer maps that fill up with duplicate entries that are never read.

There are two separate causes.

#### 1. Dynamic serializer maps never hit for `EnumSet` values (main cause)

When a serializer is found for a value whose type needs specializing, it is stored in `PropertySerializerMap` under the **specialized type's raw class**:

```java
// PropertySerializerMap.findAndAddPrimarySerializer(JavaType, ...) / findAndAddSecondarySerializer(JavaType, ...)
return new SerializerAndMapResult(serializer, newWith(type.getRawClass(), serializer));
```

but later looked up by the **runtime class**, via `serializerFor(value.getClass())`.

For `EnumSet` these never match. `TypeFactory.constructSpecializedType()` deliberately returns the base type for `EnumSet` subclasses:

```java
// 29-Oct-2015, tatu: One further shortcut: there are variants of `EnumSet`,
//    but they are impl details and we basically do not care...
if (rawBase == EnumSet.class) {
    return baseType;
}
```

So the entry is stored under `EnumSet.class`, while every lookup asks for `RegularEnumSet` (or `JumboEnumSet`). Every lookup misses: the `EnumSetSerializer` is looked up and contextualized again for every value, and another duplicate entry is added to the map (until the 8-entry limit).

This affects every path that specializes the type before calling `_findAndAddDynamic()`:

- `BeanPropertyWriter._findAndAddDynamic()` (a POJO property of type `EnumSet<X>`, `EnumSet<?>` or raw `EnumSet`)
- `AsArraySerializerBase` (e.g. `List<EnumSet<X>>`) and `MapSerializer` (e.g. `Map<String, EnumSet<X>>`), when the element/value type has generic types

It applies whether or not the enum type is statically known: `EnumSet<ABC>` is affected the same way.

#### 2. `EnumSetSerializer` does not check its own dynamic map

`EnumSetSerializer.serializeContents()` calls `_findAndAddDynamic()` without first checking `_dynamicValueSerializers.serializerFor()`, whenever `_elementSerializer` is `null` (the enum type is not known statically, e.g. a root-level `EnumSet` value):

```java
for (Enum<?> en : value) {
    if (enumSer == null) {
        enumSer = _findAndAddDynamic(ctxt, en.getDeclaringClass());
    }
    ...
```

So each `serializeContents()` call (once per non-empty set) looks up and contextualizes the enum serializer again. It also adds another entry for the same enum type each time, until the map has 8 entries and stops accepting new ones (`emptyForProperties()` does not reset when full). Other `AsArraySerializerBase` subtypes call `serializerFor()` before `_findAndAddDynamic()`.

For `EnumSet` properties this is usually hidden by cause (1): each value gets a freshly contextualized `EnumSetSerializer` with an empty map anyway. It shows up on its own for root-level values, where the same cached `EnumSetSerializer` instance is reused across writes.

### Version information

`3.x` (3.4.0-SNAPSHOT). Verified with a test.

In 2.x, `EnumSetSerializer` calls `findContentValueSerializer()` directly on every call, without the dynamic map: re-resolution happens there too, but without the duplicate map entries. The `TypeFactory` shortcut and `PropertySerializerMap` keying are the same in 2.x, so cause (1) very likely applies there too (not verified).

### To Reproduce

Register a serializer for an enum that counts `createContextual()` calls:

```java
enum ABC { A, B, C }

static class WildcardHolder { public EnumSet<?> values = EnumSet.of(ABC.A, ABC.B); }
static class TypedHolder { public EnumSet<ABC> values = EnumSet.of(ABC.A, ABC.B); }
static class ListOfSets { public List<EnumSet<ABC>> sets = List.of(EnumSet.of(ABC.A), EnumSet.of(ABC.B), EnumSet.of(ABC.C)); }

static class CountingSerializer extends StdSerializer<ABC> {
    final AtomicInteger contextualized;
    CountingSerializer(AtomicInteger c) { super(ABC.class); contextualized = c; }

    @Override
    public ValueSerializer<?> createContextual(SerializationContext ctxt, BeanProperty property) {
        contextualized.incrementAndGet();
        return this;
    }

    @Override
    public void serialize(ABC value, JsonGenerator g, SerializationContext ctxt) {
        g.writeString(value.name());
    }
}

AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger();
ObjectMapper mapper = JsonMapper.builder()
        .addModule(new SimpleModule().addSerializer(ABC.class, new CountingSerializer(counter)))
        .build();
```

Contextualization counts measured on `3.x`:

| Input | Count | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| `List.of(new WildcardHolder(), new WildcardHolder(), new WildcardHolder())` | 3 | 1 |
| same with 20 holders | 20 | 1 |
| same with raw `EnumSet` property | 3 | 1 |
| `List.of(new TypedHolder(), new TypedHolder(), new TypedHolder())` | 3 | 1 |
| `new ListOfSets()` (3 sets) | 3 | 1 |
| same with `Map<String, EnumSet<ABC>>` (3 sets) | 3 | 1 |
| `mapper.writeValueAsString(EnumSet.of(ABC.A, ABC.B))` 20 times | 20 | 1 |
| control: `List<?>` property holding enums, 3 holders | 1 | 1 |

After the 20 root-level writes, the cached root `EnumSetSerializer`'s `_dynamicValueSerializers` is a full map (8 entries), all for `ABC`.

Fixing only (2), by checking `serializerFor()` first in `EnumSetSerializer`, brings the root-level case down to 1 but leaves the POJO property cases unchanged. Also keying `BeanPropertyWriter`'s dynamic map by the runtime class brings all the POJO property cases down to 1. The `List`/`Map` of `EnumSet` cases stay at 3 until the container serializers' paths get the same change.

### Expected behavior

Each serializer is resolved once per runtime type per serializer instance, with later values served from the dynamic serializer map.

### Additional context

- Same kind of problem as #6392 (`JsonValueSerializer`) and #6395 (`StdConvertingSerializer`).
- Likely fixes:
  - (1): store dynamic map entries under the runtime class that callers look up with (e.g. pass it to `findAndAdd*()` alongside the specialized `JavaType`), rather than under `type.getRawClass()`. That fixes all callers in one place.
  - (2): check `_dynamicValueSerializers.serializerFor(en.getDeclaringClass())` before calling `_findAndAddDynamic()` in `EnumSetSerializer`.
- As with #6392/#6395, caching means the contextual settings of the first `ObjectWriter` used stay in effect for that serializer instance; this matches how other collection serializers already behave.
